@database "llm_decisions"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_decisions.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Decision model provider"
@{h1}Decision model provider

This plugin is the client for decision models: TypeSafe's Jev, reached
through OpenRouter or TypeSafe's own endpoint; Laya, an open model run
locally with @{tt}laya-serve@{utt}; and the decision models a local Ollama
runs (Nimble, Tev1). A decision model writes no text. It
gets a piece of content and named questions, and answers each question with
a number or an option: a probability for a yes/no question, one of several
named options, or a position on an ordered scale. The
@{" decision " link decision/main} plugin gives agents tools on top of it;
this plugin has no tools, no hooks and no panel of its own.

A decision model is not a chat model. Nothing here can answer a
conversation, so this plugin serves no chat provider at all: its models live
in their own section of the configuration, beside the chat models, and are
reached by decision profile. Laya and Ollama speak the same wire as Jev, so
one client serves all of them; what differs per host is its endpoint, whether it takes a
session id, the provider name its calls are booked under, and whether it
bills (Ollama does not: its calls cost 0).

@{table}
Provider name | Default endpoint | Key from the environment | Session id sent
openrouter_decisions | https://openrouter.ai/api/alpha/decisions | OPENROUTER_API_KEY | yes
systemone_decisions | https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone | TYPESAFE_API_KEY | no
ollama_decisions | http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1/systemone | (none, local) | no
@{body}

A local Laya is @{tt}systemone_decisions@{utt} with the url of the machine
that runs it.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Retries, timeouts and cancelling  " link retries}
  @{"  Usage, cost and the hooks  " link usage}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Questions and answers  " link questions}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds the plugin by its @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}
(@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt}, @{tt}provides_decisions =
["openrouter_decisions", "systemone_decisions",
"ollama_decisions"]@{utt}, no @{tt}provides@{utt}) and imports
@{tt}provider.py@{utt} the first time a decision model entry names one of
them. There is no entry in
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} and no allowlist line.

@{h2}The entries

Decision models sit under @{tt}llm_system.decision_models@{utt}, profiles
under @{tt}llm_system.decision_profiles@{utt}, and
@{tt}default_decision_profile@{utt} names the one used when a caller names
none (see @{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}). The shipped
entries, from @{tt}config/llm_openrouter.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
  decision_models:
    jev:
      provider: openrouter_decisions
      model: ~typesafe/jev-latest

  decision_profiles:
    jev:
      model_ref: jev
      description: "TypeSafe Jev - decides, does not write"

  default_decision_profile: jev
@{body}

and from @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}, a local Laya:

@{code yaml}
  decision_models:
    laya:
      provider: systemone_decisions
      model: laya-multilingual
      url: http://127.0.0.1:8788/v1/systemone
      # api_key: ${LAYA_API_KEY}

  decision_profiles:
    laya:
      model_ref: laya
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
provider | openrouter_decisions | which host (table in @{" the main page " link main})
model | (required) | sent as @{tt}model@{utt}; also the name the usage tracker prices by
url | the provider's endpoint | the WHOLE endpoint; nothing is appended to it
api_key | (from the environment) | see below
request_timeout | 60 | seconds; see @{" Retries " link retries}
max_retries | 2 | further attempts after the first; not below 0
@{body}

An unknown key in an entry stops the configuration from loading -- a
@{tt}base_url@{utt} written out of habit would otherwise be dropped and the
request would go to the default host.

@{h2}The key

A configured @{tt}api_key@{utt} always wins. Without one, the key follows
the host of the url actually called, not the provider name:
@{tt}openrouter.ai@{utt} takes @{tt}OPENROUTER_API_KEY@{utt},
@{tt}api.typesafe.ai@{utt} takes @{tt}TYPESAFE_API_KEY@{utt}. A local url (a
loopback or private address, a host name without a dot, @{tt}.local@{utt})
gets no key and no @{tt}Authorization@{utt} header. Any other host needs
@{tt}api_key@{utt} in the entry; building the client fails otherwise,
naming the host, and so does a missing environment variable. Nothing is
sent in either case.

@{h2}laya-serve

Laya runs in its own environment (it brings torch and transformers):

@{code}
pip install "laya[serve]"
LAYA_HOST=127.0.0.1 LAYA_PORT=8788 laya-serve
@{body}

Its default port is 8000, the port of this server's API, and it binds to
all interfaces by default: set both. Started with @{tt}LAYA_API_KEY@{utt},
it refuses every request (HTTP 401) until the entry names that key. It
does not refuse an unknown @{tt}model@{utt} but picks a checkpoint itself,
and its answer names @{tt}laya-rl-agent@{utt} whatever judged: name the
checkpoint in the entry.

@{h2}Ollama

Ollama serves decision models from version 0.35 on, at
@{tt}/v1/systemone@{utt} on its usual port. Pull one --
@{tt}ollama pull nimble@{utt} (9B, Bespoke Labs), @{tt}tev1@{utt} (4B) or
@{tt}tev1:0.8b@{utt} (Together AI) -- and name it:

@{code yaml}
  decision_models:
    nimble:
      provider: ollama_decisions
      model: nimble
      # url: http://192.168.1.10:11434/v1/systemone   # Ollama elsewhere

  decision_profiles:
    nimble:
      model_ref: nimble
@{body}

No key is sent to this computer or the local network; an address that is
not local (a Tailscale 100.64/10 address, a name with a dot other than
@{tt}host.docker.internal@{utt} or one ending in @{tt}.local@{utt}) needs
@{tt}api_key:@{utt} like every host (see "The key") -- any value: Ollama
ignores it, a proxy in front of it may not. Ollama's own limits, from its
documentation: a request
body of at most 64 KiB (HTTP 413 beyond), at most 64 questions, 2 to 26
options or levels, and the whole prompt within the model's context -- 8,192
tokens for Nimble, about 2,000 for Tev1 -- with the state and every question
in it. The @{" decision " link decision/main} tool lets an item run to
50,000 characters by default: with these models keep items short. How long the model stays loaded after a call is Ollama's
setting (@{tt}OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE@{utt}, 5 minutes by default); the client
sends no @{tt}keep_alive@{utt}. A model name Ollama does not have is HTTP
404 -- and so is every call to an Ollama older than 0.35, which has no
@{tt}/v1/systemone@{utt} (@{tt}404 page not found@{utt}): update Ollama, not
the model. A chat model is HTTP 400, as is a prompt beyond the loaded
context window; the @{tt}error@{utt} in the answer names which. Neither is
tried again.

@{h2}Dependencies and data

@{tt}httpx@{utt} only. The plugin is kept apart from the OpenRouter chat
plugin on purpose: that one pulls in the OpenRouter SDK, and this client
must not depend on it. Certificates are always checked. Nothing is stored;
a changed entry reaches a caller when it builds its client again.
@endnode

@node questions "Questions and answers"
@{h1}Questions and answers

@{h2}Getting a client

@{code python}
from agent_system.llm.decisions import create_decisions_from_profile

client = create_decisions_from_profile(settings, "jev")  # None: the default profile
result = await client.decide(
    "rm -rf /tmp/build",
    {"safe": {"type": "noul", "instructions": "Is this safe to run?"}},
    cancellation_token=token, session_id=session_id)
result["safe"].value   # a probability from 0 to 1
@{body}

Outside a configuration (a script, a test) the client takes its arguments
directly: @{tt}DecisionsClient(model=..., api_key=None, url=None,
request_timeout=60, max_retries=2, host=OPENROUTER)@{utt} from
@{tt}plugins.llm_decisions.system_one@{utt}; @{tt}SYSTEM_ONE@{utt} and
@{tt}OLLAMA@{utt} are the other hosts.

@{h2}decide(state, questions, *, cancellation_token=None, session_id=None)

@{tt}state@{utt} is the content to judge: text, or a JSON object or array
when its parts have names. It must not be empty; bytes, numbers and other
types are refused. @{tt}session_id@{utt} groups the call in the hooks, and
at OpenRouter also in their logs; it never reaches the model.

@{tt}questions@{utt} maps a name to a question. The client checks them
before anything is sent (@{tt}check_questions@{utt}, callable on its own):

@{table}
type | criteria | The answer's value
noul | optional; when given, a mapping such as {"true": "...", "false": "..."} | a probability from 0 to 1
choice | required; a mapping of at least two options to what each means | the chosen option's name
score | required; a list of at least two levels, lowest first | a position on the scale, counted from 0
@{body}

Every question needs non-empty @{tt}instructions@{utt}. The minimum of two
options or levels is this client's own rule, not the endpoint's.

A score is a point, not a rung: on @{tt}["none", "low", "high"]@{utt}, 0 is
none, 2 is high, and 0.58 lies between none and low.

@{h2}The result

@{table}
Field | Meaning
answers | per question name an Answer; @{tt}result[name]@{utt} reads it
model | the model that answered, as the host names it; the configured name when the answer carries none
provider | the backend the gateway routed to (OpenRouter sends it), else None
id | the host's call id, else None
input_tokens, output_tokens | from the answer's usage; 0 when absent
cost | the cost the answer reports; 0 on Ollama; otherwise None when it reports none -- unknown, not free
duration_ms | from the first attempt to the answer
@{body}

@{table}
Answer field | Meaning
name, type | the question's name and type
value | the probability, the option, or the position
confidence | when the model sends it, else None
probabilities | a choice: per option; a score: per position, as text ({"0": 0.51, "1": 0.39}); else None
legend | a score: position to level name ({"0": "none", ...}); else None
@{body}

Jev sends a noul answer as the probability alone; confidence and
probabilities come with choice and score. Answers to names that were not
asked are kept; fields a host adds of its own (laya-serve's
@{tt}answer_confidence@{utt}, @{tt}action@{utt}, @{tt}routing@{utt}) are
not read. The recorded answers of OpenRouter and laya-serve are in the
plugin's tests; Ollama's answer there is the one of its documentation.
@endnode

@node retries "Retries, timeouts and cancelling"
@{h1}Retries, timeouts and cancelling

@{tt}request_timeout@{utt} is handed to httpx as the timeout of each
attempt: connecting, sending, and each wait for data may take that many
seconds.

A call makes at most @{tt}max_retries@{utt} + 1 attempts. Another attempt
follows a connection error, a timeout, and the statuses 429, 500, 502, 503,
504, 520 to 524 and 529. Before it the client waits:

@{bullet}the host's @{tt}Retry-After@{utt}, when it is a number of seconds
from 0 to 60;
@{bullet}otherwise 2 seconds times the attempt number (2, 4, ...) -- also
when @{tt}Retry-After@{utt} is a date, negative or not a number.

A @{tt}Retry-After@{utt} above 60 seconds ends the call at once with "Decisions
API busy (HTTP <status>), asks to retry after <n>s": sitting out the wait
inside a tool call helps nobody. Any other status from 400 up, and an answer
this client cannot read, are not tried again.

A cancel ends the call wherever it is: before an attempt (nothing is sent),
while a request is in flight (the request is stopped), or during the wait
between two attempts. It arrives as @{tt}asyncio.CancelledError("Request
cancelled by user")@{utt}.
@endnode

@node usage "Usage, cost and the hooks"
@{h1}Usage, cost and the hooks

A decision call belongs to no agent, so the client reports to the global
hook registry itself (@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks}). Every context
carries @{tt}agent@{utt} empty, the provider name as
@{tt}llm_provider@{utt} and @{tt}agent_name@{utt}, the configured model as
@{tt}llm_model@{utt}, the url, the session id and the id of the run the
call is made in.

@{table}
When | Hook | What it carries
the call starts (after the questions are checked) | pre_llm_request | the request body
an attempt failed and another follows | post_llm_response | error "[RETRY <n>/<total>] <reason>", finish reason retry, no usage
an answer | post_llm_response | usage {input_tokens, output_tokens, cost}, the answer values, finish reason stop, the served model as metadata served_by
any other end (error, cancel) | post_llm_response | error "<type>: <message>", finish reason error; the usage only when an answer came back and was refused
@{body}

So each call ends with exactly one report that is not a retry. A hook that
raises never breaks the call; the first such failure per provider and phase
is logged as a warning, later ones only at debug level.

The usage tracker books agent-less reports that carry a usage
(@{" What it records " link context_usage_tracker/hooks}), under the
provider name. The cost the answer reports wins. An Ollama call costs 0:
Ollama bills nothing, so no model needs a price row. A call of another host
that reports none -- a local Laya, TypeSafe's own endpoint -- the tracker prices from
@{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt} by the entry's @{tt}model@{utt}; without
a row there, the cost stays unknown. The message debugger records every
report (@{" The capture hooks " link message_debugger/hooks}), the served
model included.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

Refused before anything is sent, as @{tt}ValueError@{utt} (no hook fires):

@{table}
Message begins | When
A decisions request needs at least one question | no questions
Question '<name>' must be a mapping | a question is not an object
Question '<name>' has type ... | a type other than noul, choice, score
Question '<name>' has no instructions | instructions missing or empty
Question '<name>' is a score: criteria must be an ordered list ... | score criteria not a list of two or more
Question '<name>' is a choice: criteria must be a mapping ... | choice criteria not a mapping of two or more
Question '<name>' is a noul: criteria is optional, but ... | noul criteria that is not a mapping
A decisions state is text, an object or an array | bytes, a number, ...
A decisions request needs a state | an empty state
@{body}

Everything the endpoint does wrong is a @{tt}DecisionsError@{utt}. Its
@{tt}usage@{utt} is set when an answer came back with a usage and was
refused: the call was billed (its cost as in the result), and the
@{" decision " link decision/main} tool counts it in its totals.

@{table}
Message begins | When | usage
Decisions API error <status>: <body> | a status from 400 up that is not retried | none
Decisions call failed: ... | the last attempt failed with a retryable status, a connection error or a timeout | none
Decisions API busy (HTTP <status>), asks to retry after <n>s | a Retry-After above 60 seconds | none
Decisions API answered <status> with no JSON | the body is not JSON | none
Decisions API answered <status> with <type>, not an object | JSON, but not an object | none
Decisions API answered a usage this client cannot read | usage figures that are not numbers | none
Decisions API answered <type> where the answers object belongs | answers is not an object | when reported
Decisions answer '<name>' has type ... with no usable value | an answer of an unknown type, not an object, or without its value | when reported
Decisions API left <names> unanswered | a question got no answer | when reported
@{body}

Bodies quoted in a message are cut to a few hundred characters.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

Exactly the request body, and nothing about the run:

@{code json}
{"model": "~typesafe/jev-latest",
 "state": "rm -rf /tmp/build",
 "questions": {"safe": {"type": "noul", "instructions": "Is this safe to run?"}}}
@{body}

plus @{tt}session_id@{utt} at OpenRouter, which only groups their logs. The
questionnaire is most of the input: a one-line state with three questions
counts a few hundred input tokens, so the number of questions weighs more
in the price than the length of the content. There is no conversation and
no prompt cache.

Jev is not deterministic -- the same question can come back a few
hundredths apart -- and Jev, Laya and Nimble give different numbers for the
same question: a threshold tuned on one does not carry over to another.
@endnode
